**Principle of selection sort.** For each position `i`, find the smallest element among the positions `i` to the end (`min` keeps its index), then swap it with the element at position `i`. After the pass for `i`, the elements at positions `0 .. i` are in their final place.

**A defect of Figure 1 as printed.** In the figure the three lines of the swap (`tmp <- a[i]`, `a[i] <- a[min]`, `a[min] <- tmp`) are indented *inside* the loop on `j`, before its `endFor`. A literal translation swaps at every step of the inner loop, and this does **not** sort the names of Table 1: a Python translation of that version returns `Bart, Bert, Cain, Chang, Gary, Jeff, Lara, Jill, Mary, ...` (Lara before Jill). The comments of the figure ("swap the minimum element with the current element") and the principle above show that the swap must be done **once per value of i, after the loop on j**; this is the version below.

**Algorithm (0-based indices, as in C).**

```
procedure select_sort (Array, size)
    for i from 0 to size - 2
        min <- i                          // assume Array[i] is the smallest
        for j from i + 1 to size - 1
            if Array[j] < Array[min] then
                min <- j                  // a smaller element is found
            endIf
        endFor
        tmp <- Array[i]                   // swap Array[i] and Array[min]
        Array[i] <- Array[min]
        Array[min] <- tmp
    endFor
endProcedure
```

The pseudocode loops `1 .. sizeOfArray - 1` and `i+1 .. sizeOfArray` become `0 .. size - 2` and `i + 1 .. size - 1` because C arrays start at index 0.

**ANSI C.** Strings cannot be compared with `<` or copied with `=`: the library functions `strcmp` (negative result if the first string comes first in alphabetical order) and `strcpy` (from `<string.h>`) are used.

```c
void select_sort(char array[][MAXLEN], int size)
{
    int  i, j, min;
    char tmp[MAXLEN];

    for (i = 0; i < size - 1; i++) {
        min = i;                                  /* index of the smallest name so far */
        for (j = i + 1; j < size; j++)
            if (strcmp(array[j], array[min]) < 0)
                min = j;
        strcpy(tmp, array[i]);                    /* swap array[i] and array[min] */
        strcpy(array[i], array[min]);
        strcpy(array[min], tmp);
    }
}
```

