**Marking:** the program file saved with the requested name and printed (1).

**Principle.** Binary search works only on a **sorted** array (this is why the names are sorted first). The procedure looks at the middle element `mid` of the range `min .. max`: if it is the name, the search stops; if the middle element comes before the name, the name can only be in the right half (`mid + 1 .. max`); otherwise it can only be in the left half (`min .. mid - 1`). When the range is empty (`min > max`) the name is not in the array. Each call halves the range, so at most about log2(15), that is 4, names are examined for 15 names.

Because `bin_search_rec` returns nothing, it reports its result through the **global variables** `found` (was the name found?) and `position` (its index), which `bin_search` reads after the call. The local variables `bottom` and `top` of Figure 2 only copy `min` and `max`.

**Algorithm (0-based indices).**

```
found <- false ; position <- 0                  // global variables

procedure bin_search_rec (Array, name, min, max)
    bottom <- min ; top <- max
    if min <= max then
        mid <- (min + max) div 2
        if Array[mid] = name then
            found <- true ; position <- mid
        else if Array[mid] < name then
            bin_search_rec (Array, name, mid + 1, top)     // right half
        else
            bin_search_rec (Array, name, bottom, mid - 1)  // left half
        endIf
    endIf
endProcedure

procedure bin_search (Array, name, min, max)
    found <- false
    bin_search_rec (Array, name, min, max)
    if found = false then print "Name not found"
    else print Array[position]
endProcedure
```

**ANSI C.** `strcmp(a, b)` returns 0 if the strings are equal, a negative number if `a` comes before `b`, a positive number otherwise. `(min + max) / 2` is an integer division because both operands are `int`.

```c
int found = 0;      /* global: 1 when the name has been found */
int position = 0;   /* global: index of the name in the array (0-based) */

void bin_search_rec(char array[][MAXLEN], const char *name, int min, int max)
{
    int bottom = min;
    int top    = max;
    int mid;

    if (min <= max) {
        mid = (min + max) / 2;
        if (strcmp(array[mid], name) == 0) {
            found = 1;
            position = mid;
        } else if (strcmp(array[mid], name) < 0)
            bin_search_rec(array, name, mid + 1, top);      /* right half */
        else
            bin_search_rec(array, name, bottom, mid - 1);   /* left half */
    }
}

void bin_search(char array[][MAXLEN], const char *name, int min, int max)
{
    found = 0;                       /* a new search starts: nothing found yet */
    bin_search_rec(array, name, min, max);
    if (found == 0)
        printf("Name not found\n");
    else
        printf("%s\n", array[position]);
}
```

For the sorted array of (iii), `bin_search(names, "Mary", 0, N - 1)` examines in turn index 7 (Lara, before Mary: go right), index 11 (Nora, after Mary: go left), index 9 (Neba, after Mary: go left), index 8 (Mary: found), so `found = 1`, `position = 8` and "Mary" is printed. For "Zack" the calls examine indexes 7, 11, 13, 14 (all before "Zack"), then the range becomes `15 .. 14`, empty: `found` stays 0 and "Name not found" is printed. Both traces were confirmed with a Python translation of the two procedures.

*Remark.* The statement `found = 0;` in `bin_search` is not in Figure 2 (the figure sets `found` to false once, at the start of the program); it is harmless for one search and makes the procedure correct if it is called several times.

**Marking:** header of bin_search with array, name, min and max (1); call of bin_search_rec with matching arguments (1); the name printed when found (1); the message "Name not found" otherwise, with the global variables used (1).

The main program of (iii) already sorts the array; it now also asks the user for a name and passes it to `bin_search` with the bounds of the whole array (`0` and `N - 1`).

```c
#define INPUTLEN 20     /* room for a typed name, longer than any name of Table 1 */

int main(void)
{
    char names[N][MAXLEN] = { "Bert", "Jeff", "Gary", "Neba", "Chang",
                              "Olga", "Nora", "Mary", "Vera", "Rani",
                              "Neil", "Jill", "Cain", "Lara", "Bart" };
    char name[INPUTLEN];

    printf("Names before sorting:\n");
    doDisplay(names, N);
    select_sort(names, N);            /* binary search needs a sorted array */
    printf("Names after sorting:\n");
    doDisplay(names, N);

    printf("Enter the name to search for: ");
    scanf("%19s", name);              /* at most 19 characters, no overflow */
    bin_search(names, name, 0, N - 1);
    return 0;
}
```

Points to note:

- `select_sort` must be called **before** `bin_search`: on an unsorted array the binary search can miss a name that is present.
- The buffer `name` is larger (`INPUTLEN`) than `MAXLEN`, so that a long typed name is simply "not found" instead of overflowing the buffer, and `"%19s"` limits the number of characters read.
- The comparison is **case-sensitive**: "mary" is not found, "Mary" is (confirmed with the Python translation). A more tolerant program would convert the typed name to the case used in Table 1 before searching.

**Marking:** `select_sort` called before the search (1); a prompt displayed and the name read into a suitable variable (1); `bin_search` called with the array, the name and the bounds 0 and N - 1 (1); program consistent with (iii) (1).

